WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.682
Many C++ teams support more than one compiler, operating system, architecture, or build mode.

2
00:00:05.682 --> 00:00:11.770
A strong hardening strategy begins with security intent rather than a memorized list of flags.

3
00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:19.076
Define the category first: diagnostics, stack protection, binary layout, memory permissions, control-flow protection, sanitizer testing, or runtime

4
00:00:19.076 --> 00:00:19.482
checks.

5
00:00:19.482 --> 00:00:23.946
GCC, Clang, and MSVC do not always map one-to-one.

6
00:00:23.946 --> 00:00:28.817
Similar options may differ in behavior, maturity, defaults, dependencies, or supported platforms.

7
00:00:28.817 --> 00:00:34.905
A flag that is useful on one target may be unavailable or inappropriate on another.

8
00:00:34.905 --> 00:00:39.775
Teams should verify against the selected compiler, version, platform, and build system.

9
00:00:39.775 --> 00:00:42.211
Build profiles help organize the strategy.

10
00:00:42.211 --> 00:00:45.458
Debug builds may favor diagnostics and library checks.

11
00:00:45.458 --> 00:00:48.299
Sanitizer builds add runtime instrumentation for tests.

12
00:00:48.299 --> 00:00:51.952
Staging builds may closely resemble release with extra observability.

13
00:00:51.952 --> 00:00:55.604
Release builds emphasize verified hardening, compatibility, and performance requirements.

14
00:00:55.604 --> 00:01:01.287
Documentation should include compiler versions, flag rationale, profile definitions, exceptions, and known compatibility issues.

15
00:01:01.287 --> 00:01:06.969
This documentation should live close enough to the build system that it stays current.

16
00:01:06.969 --> 00:01:13.463
A stale wiki is less useful than a checked-in hardening matrix reviewed with build changes.

17
00:01:13.463 --> 00:01:17.927
The hardening matrix should be revisited as platforms and dependencies change.

18
00:01:17.927 --> 00:01:21.580
New compiler versions may add features or change defaults.

19
00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:23.610
Dependencies may require compatibility exceptions.

20
00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:26.451
Operating systems may expose new protection mechanisms.

21
00:01:26.451 --> 00:01:28.480
Governance keeps the strategy alive.
